Description: Read-only Linux hardening scanner
 Plumbline audits a Linux host's configuration against a hardening baseline and
 reports what has drifted, with the evidence behind each verdict attached and an
 explicit account of whatever it could not read. Every operation that touches
 the operating system passes through a single Go interface seam, so no check
 opens a file, runs a command or reads /proc on its own; an upstream lint rule
 enforces that boundary, which is what makes the audit path auditable in turn.
 The scanner therefore only observes the host and never alters it: where a
 failing check can be repaired, Plumbline prints or writes an idempotent shell
 script for an operator to review and run, rather than applying the change
 itself. Findings are emitted as a terminal report, as JSON or as SARIF for a
 code-scanning dashboard, and the exit code distinguishes a host that failed its
 gates from one the scanner could not fully see.
